text:
lee: Heads-up — onboarding sample app failing in Vite + Edge worker due to types + import shape. Anyone seen `generic` types collapse to `any` after bundling?

anya: Yup. Ran into this yesterday. Overloads get flattened by esbuild and TS picks a broad signature. Error was 'No overload matches this call' in editor, then runtime 401 because auth wrapper wasn't applied.

sam: Can you paste a tiny repro? which sdk version?

lee: Using @redwood/inference@0.9.1 (esm). Minimal snippet:

```ts
// src/client.ts
import Redwood from '@redwood/inference';
const client = new Redwood.Client<{ foo: string }>({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY });
```

lee: tsserver shows the generic fine, but after Vite build the emitted bundle has a different call signature and `client.request` loses type params.

jordan: FYI edge runtimes don't run our node auth plugin. If the bundler switches to the CJS entry, the auth pipeline isn't wired the same way -> 401s on retry. So this is both a typing + runtime parity bug.

tyler: Two quick notes: 1) tsconfig flags like `preserveValueImports` and `moduleResolution` can change how named vs default imports are resolved. 2) We publish both ESM and CJS types; some bundlers prefer the ESM entry but still rewrite imports oddly.

anya: Workaround that worked for me: use named import + explicit type param on the class: 

```ts
import { Client } from '@redwood/inference';
const client = new Client<{ foo: string }>({ apiKey: RW_KEY });
```

anya: Also add "moduleResolution": "node" + "importsNotUsedAsValues": "preserve" in tsconfig for Vite.

lee: Named import fixes TS but when I switched to it in the sample app the edge bundle errored with "default is not a function" in local dev for another project. so it's a bit of a tradeoff depending on consumer toolchain.

sam: Agreed. We should: 1) update the onboarding sample to recommend the named import for Edge/Vite, 2) add note about tsconfig flags, 3) add an edge auth fallback so runtime doesn't rely on node-only middleware. I'll open a PR for the docs + sample.

jordan: :thumbsup: Also suggest adding a tiny CI test that builds the sample with Vite + esbuild target=es2022 and checks types with `tsc --noEmit`. That would catch regressions.

lee: For immediate unblock I added a local declaration in the repo: 

```ts
// global.d.ts (hacky temp)
declare module '@redwood/inference' {
  export class Client<T = any> {
    constructor(opts: any);
  }
}
```

tyler: Filed DEVEX-487 with reproduction steps + suggested fixes (docs PR + runtime auth fallback). If anyone wants to take the docs PR ping me and I can assign.

lee: I'll attach the gist with the minimal repro in a sec. Thanks all :)
